## A page you can touch

In the lesson on rendering you saw that the browser reads HTML and builds a **DOM** (Document Object Model) from it: a tree in which every tag, every piece of text and every attribute becomes a node. The page on screen is drawn from this tree. What matters for us: the browser hands the tree to JavaScript. In a script it’s available under the name `document`, and whatever the script changes in it, the browser redraws at once.

Every kind of interactivity works this way: a menu opens because a script removed the `hidden` attribute from an element; a counter grows because a script wrote a new number into an element; a list appears because a script created an element for each item and put it on the page. Three actions: **find**, **read or change**, **create and insert**.

## Finding an element

The language has two commands for searching, and they take the same **selectors** you wrote in CSS: `h3`, `.card`, `#offer`, `nav a`.

```js
const first = document.querySelector('.card');          // the first match, or null
const all = document.querySelectorAll('#offer .card');  // every match
```

`querySelector` returns one element, or `null` if nothing was found. `querySelectorAll` returns a **list** of nodes (a `NodeList`), possibly empty. You can walk it with `forEach` or `for...of`, and to use `map` and `filter` you turn it into an array: `Array.from(all)`.

From a found element you can search further: `card.querySelector('h3')` looks only inside that card. That lets you write shorter selectors, and the script comes out tidier.

Try selectors on a small page, and what you find is highlighted in both the markup and the tree:

<DomTree />

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: what does querySelector return if there’s no such element?</summary>

`null`. So when an element may be missing, check the result, or the next line, such as `el.textContent = ...`, will fail with an error.

</details>

## Reading and changing

Once an element is found, it has properties and methods:

- `textContent` reads and writes the element’s **text**: `title.textContent = 'New title'`;
- `getAttribute('href')` and `setAttribute('href', '#top')` work with attributes. Many attributes also have short properties: `link.href`, `input.value`, `el.id`, `el.hidden`;
- `classList.add('selected')`, `classList.remove('selected')` and `classList.toggle('selected')` manage classes. This is the best way to change how something looks: the script attaches a class, and CSS knows what to do with it. The styling stays in CSS and the logic in JS;
- `dataset` gives access to attributes like `data-price="1200"`: `card.dataset.price`. That’s how markup passes its own data to a script.

## Creating and inserting

To add something new, you first create an element in memory, set it up, and only then put it in the tree:

```js
const row = document.createElement('li');   // not on the page yet
row.textContent = 'A new item';
list.append(row);                           // now it is
```

Other commands you’ll need: `prepend` (to the start), `remove()` (take an element away) and `replaceChildren(...)` (replace all the contents). If you insert many elements in a row, build them in memory and insert them with a single `append` with several arguments: the browser then doesn’t have to redraw the page after each one.

## Why other people’s text must not go in through innerHTML

There’s a shorter way too: the `innerHTML` property. You write a string of markup into it, and the browser parses it:

```js
box.innerHTML = '<p>Hello, ' + name + '</p>';
```

It looks convenient, but if `name` came from a visitor (from a form field, a comment, a server’s answer, the page’s address), it may not be a name at all, but markup with an event handler. The browser sees no difference between “your” markup and “someone else’s”: it parses everything and runs the stranger’s code in the name of your site. This vulnerability is called **XSS** (cross-site scripting). It’s how sessions are stolen and pages are replaced.

The rule is simple: **other people’s text goes in through `textContent`**. It writes the string as text, the characters `<` and `>` stay ordinary letters, and nothing runs. Keep `innerHTML` for markup that you wrote in full yourself.

Try the same string both ways:

<DomTree mode="inject" />

## When a script runs

A mistake everyone trips over: a script looks for an element, and it isn’t there yet. An ordinary `<script>` stops the parsing of the page and runs at once, so everything lower in the document is still invisible to it. There are three ways out: put the script at the very end of the page, give it the `defer` attribute, or make it a module, as in our editor (`type="module"`). A module runs after the page is parsed, and you can search for anything in it.

## Practice

A 2014 site. You already have a list of cards, but on a long page they can’t all be seen on the first screen. Let a script build a **quick jump** from the cards: a link to every offer at the top of the section, plus a counter.

The script invents nothing, it reads what is in the markup: it finds the cards, takes the heading of each and makes a link to it. Add one more card to the markup, and a link appears by itself. And if there are no cards, the visitor sees a clear message rather than an empty space.

Notice what is missing from the task: the word `innerHTML`. A card’s heading can contain anything, so everything goes through `textContent`.

## What was wrong with the old DOM

There was a time when the DOM differed from browser to browser: Internet Explorer had some methods, Netscape had others, and `document.all` and `document.layers` had to be guessed at. A standard appeared in the late 1990s, and the browsers were slow to follow it. That confusion is why the **jQuery** library was born in 2006: one function, `$('.card')`, that worked the same in every browser. Millions of sites were built on it, and many still run on it, so if you read someone else’s code and see a dollar sign, you’re looking at jQuery.

Today browsers behave alike, the standard is called the DOM Standard (WHATWG), and `querySelector`, `classList` and `append` do the jobs for which a library used to be added. New sites don’t need jQuery: it weighs tens of kilobytes and solves problems that no longer exist. What stayed right is something else: find elements with selectors, change classes rather than styles, and never trust other people’s text.
